{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ tenant.name }}{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2 justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
			<h1 class="h3 mb-0">{{ tenant.name }}</h1>

			{#
				The way back, and it is here rather than only inside the panels
				for one reason (XIV-66): a person may untick every box, and an
				empty layout is a real answer that has to stay recoverable. A
				link that lived among the widgets would disappear with them and
				leave "ask an administrator" as the only escape — which is the
				outcome the three-layer chain exists to prevent.
			#}
			<a class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" href="{{ path('account') }}#dashboard">
				{{ 'dashboard.customise'|trans }}
			</a>
		</div>

		{#
			The page is its widgets and nothing else (XIV-81). What used to be here
			— a tile grid and two empty states — is `dashboard/widget/modules.html.twig`
			now, which is what makes follow-ups *a* widget rather than the one thing
			that got special treatment. Anything the landing page grows next is a
			class implementing DashboardWidget and a template of its own.

			Which panels these are, and in what order, is the reader's own layout
			over the tenant's default over the priority the tags declare (XIV-66),
			all of it already applied by the time they arrive. Nothing here sorts,
			filters or knows which widget is which.

			`only`, so a panel is rendered with exactly what its widget handed over
			and cannot quietly start depending on a variable that happens to be in
			scope on this page. The globals every template has — `chrome`, `app` —
			are unaffected.
		#}
		{% for panel in panels %}
			<section class="mb-4">
				{% if panel.heading %}
					<h2 class="h5 mb-3">{{ panel.nameKey|trans({}, panel.domain) }}</h2>
				{% endif %}

				{% if panel.defer %}
					{#
						Fetched in a request of its own, so a widget that counts
						rows costs its own tile rather than the whole landing page
						— the worst possible place for a page whose cost is the
						sum of its parts, since it is the first thing every user
						loads after signing in.

						`defer` rather than `lazy`: these cards are above the fold
						on any screen worth designing for, so waiting for them to
						scroll into view would be a delay with nothing bought by
						it. `lazy` is the right answer for the widget that
						eventually sits at the bottom of a long dashboard.
					#}
					<twig:DashboardPanel
						widget="{{ panel.key }}"
						loading="defer"
						loading-template="dashboard/_loading.html.twig"
					/>
				{% else %}
					{{ include(panel.template, panel.values, with_context = false) }}
				{% endif %}
			</section>
		{% endfor %}
	</main>
{% endblock %}
